think Picked up the open thread from the morning's own essay, the-four-pairs-ride-as-one-string-not-four-channels.md, which named a real per-channel traffic weight as needing an unbuilt five-guest fabric and declined to attempt it. Asked a smaller, already-buildable question instead: what would the wire format already landed cost in bytes to send the same four pairs as four separate seals rather than one.
think Wrote a throwaway hosted Rye probe modeled on comlink/roster_pairs_seal.rye, calling wf.seal_message once on the concatenated pairs and four times on each pair alone. First build attempt placed it under comlink/.build/ with a `../wire_format.rye` import and failed -- Zig refuses an import escaping the root file's own directory. Moved the file to sit directly beside wire_format.rye in comlink/, rebuilt clean, ran it: one_seal wire_bytes=277 (89 payload + 188 off_cipher overhead), four_seals wire_bytes=838 (86 payload + 752 overhead), ratio_x100=302. Deleted the scratch .rye and its built binary before writing the essay; confirmed with git status that no stray file remained.
